  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 AM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nLake effect showers are expected to end early this morning as\ninversion lower in the wake of the departing mid-upper level\ntrough. Lake convergence band will gradually shift west this\nmorning with lake effect cloudiness spreading into portions of\nnortheast IL toward sunrise. Lake cloudiness is expected to\ndecrease later this morning with sunny skies expected elsewhere. \nThe sunshine and MUCH lighter winds will make today's highs in \nthe 50s feel milder than Friday's 50s. \n\nEarly this evening, high pressure will be centered over Indiana\nand eastern IL. The clear skies, dry boundary layer, and\nlight/calm winds should set the stage for strong radiational\ncooling. The high pressure system is progged to shift slowly\neast tonight, likely allowing a light south to southwest wind to\ndevelop over northern CWA, with areas east of I-55 into\nnorthwest Indiana probably remaining more decoupled with light\nor nearly calm winds until sunrise Sunday. Have sided with the\nslightly chillier MAV guidance for low temps tonight, with lows\nin the 30s away from the urban heat island. The coldest lows \nshould be east of I-55 and in the typically colder valley and \ncold air drainage areas of northern IL where lows in the low to \nmid 30s are expected. \n\nThe most favorable area for frost tonight will be east of I-55\ninto northwest Indiana and after coordination with neighboring\nWFOs, we have opted to hoist a frost advisory there. Farther\nnorth and west, there are questions about how widespread frost\nwill get given the increasing winds. Plus, some of these areas\nmay see an end to the growing season with freezing temps this\nmorning. The day shift can take a closer look at this and could\npotentially need to expand the frost advisory a bit farther\nnorth/west.\n\nOtherwise, the weather looks incredibly tranquil Sunday through\nthe upcoming work week. Many of the days one may be hard pressed\nto even spot a cloud in the sky! Temperatures Sunday will\nmoderate back closer to normal into the low-mid 60s. Monday will\nwarm a few more degrees, then by Tuesday through Saturday\nafternoon highs are largely expected to be in the 70s. This is\nparticularly impressive at a time of year when the daily average\nhighs are falling quite quickly. By the middle and end of the\nweek the average highs are in the upper 50s, so the 70s we're\nforecasting will be a good 15-20F above average!\n\n- Izzi",
